thenticator without a corresponding request. =C2=A0[...]<br>&gt;<br>&gt; Th=
is statement duplicates a requirement already stated above.<br><br>We can s=
afely remove this line.</div><div><br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt; =C2=A0 =C2=A0Cli=
entHello extensions are used to determine permissible extensions<br>&gt; =
=C2=A0 =C2=A0in the Certificate message.<br>&gt;<br>&gt; I&#39;d suggest re=
iterating the &quot;negotiation&quot; model for extensions, and that<br>&gt=
; you generally can&#39;t send something in a response that you didn&#39;t =
receive<br>&gt; in a request (with, of course, the hardcoded exceptions lis=
ted in the<br>&gt; initial spec for that functionality).<br><br>Not sure th=
e right way to word this. Here&#39;s a shot:<br>=C2=A0 =C2=A0ClientHello ex=
tensions are used to determine permissible extensions<br>=C2=A0 =C2=A0in th=
e Certificate message.=C2=A0 This follows the general model for<br>=C2=A0 =
=C2=A0extensions in TLS in which extensions can only be included<br>=C2=A0 =
=C2=A0as part of a Certificate message if they were previously sent as<br>=
=C2=A0 =C2=A0part of a CertificateRequest message or ClientHello message.<b=
r><br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt; Section 4.2.1<br>&gt;<br>&gt; =C2=A0 =C2=A0In ad=
dition to &quot;signature_algorithms&quot; and<br>&gt; =C2=A0 =C2=A0&quot;s=
ignature_algorithms_cert&quot;, the &quot;server_name&quot; [RFC6066],<br>&=
gt; =C2=A0 =C2=A0&quot;certificate_authorities&quot; (Section 4.2.4. of [TL=
S13]), and<br>&gt; =C2=A0 =C2=A0&quot;oid_filters&quot; (Section 4.2.5. of =
[TLS13]) extensions are used to<br>&gt; =C2=A0 =C2=A0guide certificate sele=
ction.=C2=A0 The extensions, or others that might<br>&gt; =C2=A0 =C2=A0affe=
ct certificate selection, are taken from the authenticator<br>&gt; =C2=A0 =
=C2=A0request if present, or the TLS handshake if not.<br>&gt;<br>&gt; The =
text about &quot;taken from the authenticator request if present, or the<br=
>&gt; TLS handshake if not&quot; seems to be duplicating previous discussio=
ns.<br>&gt;<br>&gt; Maybe go with &quot;commonly used [to guide certifciate=
 selection]&quot;?=C2=A0 We<br>&gt; don&#39;t limit what can be used for th=
at purpose in core spec, IIRC.<br><br>How about we just remove that sentenc=
e?</div><div><br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt; I&#39;m a little reluctant to bring t=
his up, as it&#39;s fairly tangential to<br>&gt; this document, but there s=
eems to be some general disagreement in the<br>&gt; community about whether=
 the SNI usage model is a proper &quot;negotiation&quot;,<br>&gt; where the=
